WHAT: exclude KCharSelect, KCharSelectData, KDateTimeEdit and KMimeTypeEditor
      (and the 3.1 MB kcharselect-data resource) from the WASI build.
WHY:  each one needs a Qt feature wk qtbase does not have, and each was a hard
      compile failure, not a warning:
        * kcharselectdata_p.h:13 "fatal error: QFuture file not found". Not a
          missing include path -- FEATURE_thread=OFF means <QFuture> is NOT
          INSTALLED. KCharSelectData builds a Unicode index in a QRunnable on
          a QThreadPool (kcharselectdata.cpp:34 `class RunIndexCreation :
          public QFutureInterface<Index>, public QRunnable`) and
          waitForFinished()es it on demand. With no threads that is a rewrite,
          not a guard. WORTH KNOWING: this is precisely why KCharSelect-the-app
          is NOT the cheap first KDE port a CMake-only reading suggests -- its
          central widget is the one thing in KWidgetsAddons that cannot exist
          here.
        * kdatetimeedit.cpp: QTimeZone::availableTimeZoneIds(),
          QTimeZone(QByteArray), QDateTime::timeZone(). FEATURE_timezone=OFF,
          and a wk node has no zone database to switch it on with.
        * kmimetypeeditor.cpp:12 "fatal error: QProcess file not found". The
          class exists to launch `keditfiletype`. FEATURE_process=OFF; there is
          no fork/exec across a WASI component boundary.
      The HEADERS are removed from the target sources as well as the .cpp
      files, otherwise AUTOMOC still parses kcharselectdata_p.h and
      mocs_compilation.cpp fails on the same missing <QFuture> even though the
      translation unit is gone.
      SAY IT PLAINLY: this REMOVES four public classes from KWidgetsAddons on
      this platform. Their headers are still installed, so a downstream user
      fails at link rather than at #include. Nothing in KCalc graph touches
      any of them (checked by grep across every framework in the graph).
UPSTREAM: not-as-is. Upstream would want these behind proper feature options
      (KWIDGETSADDONS_WITH_CHARSELECT etc.), not a platform test.
diff --git a/src/CMakeLists.txt b/src/CMakeLists.txt
index 1d041a5..c7b09ce 100644
--- a/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -202,6 +202,49 @@ target_sources(KF6WidgetsAddons PRIVATE
     icons.qrc
 )
 
+# --- WASI: four widgets this platform cannot carry --------------------------
+# Each is excluded for a NAMED missing Qt feature, not for convenience, and
+# none of them is reachable from anything else in KCalc's framework graph
+# (verified by grep across kconfigwidgets, kxmlgui, kbookmarks, kiconthemes,
+# kitemviews, kcolorscheme, knotifications and kcalc itself).
+#
+#   kcharselect + kcharselectdata   QFuture / QFutureInterface / QRunnable on a
+#       QThreadPool. wk qtbase is FEATURE_thread=OFF, so <QFuture> is not even
+#       INSTALLED ("'QFuture' file not found"). KCharSelectData builds its
+#       3 MB Unicode index on a worker thread and waitForFinished()es it on
+#       demand; with no threads that is not a guard to relax, it is a rewrite.
+#       This is why KCharSelect-the-app is NOT a cheaper first KDE port than
+#       KCalc, contrary to what a CMake-only reading suggests.
+#   kdatetimeedit                   QTimeZone::availableTimeZoneIds(),
+#       QTimeZone(QByteArray) and QDateTime::timeZone(). FEATURE_timezone=OFF,
+#       and a wk node has no zone database to turn it on with.
+#   kmimetypeeditor                 #include <QProcess> at line 12 -- it exists
+#       to launch `keditfiletype`. FEATURE_process=OFF; there is no fork/exec
+#       across a WASI component boundary.
+#
+# The headers stay installed, so a downstream user of these classes fails at
+# LINK time rather than at #include. That is a known sharp edge; it is
+# recorded in PORTING.md rather than hidden.
+if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    get_target_property(_kwa_wasi_srcs KF6WidgetsAddons SOURCES)
+    list(REMOVE_ITEM _kwa_wasi_srcs
+        kcharselect.cpp
+        kcharselectdata.cpp
+        kdatetimeedit.cpp
+        kmimetypeeditor.cpp
+        # The headers go too, or AUTOMOC still runs over them: moc parses
+        # kcharselectdata_p.h, hits its `#include <QFuture>` at line 13, and
+        # the generated mocs_compilation.cpp fails to compile even though not
+        # one of these translation units is being built any more.
+        kcharselect.h
+        kcharselect_p.h
+        kcharselectdata_p.h
+        kdatetimeedit.h
+        kmimetypeeditor.h
+    )
+    set_target_properties(KF6WidgetsAddons PROPERTIES SOURCES "${_kwa_wasi_srcs}")
+endif()
+
 set(kwidgetsaddons_UI_SRCS)
 qt_wrap_ui(kwidgetsaddons_UI_SRCS
    kdatetimeedit.ui
@@ -331,12 +374,17 @@ install(FILES
   DESTINATION ${KDE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR_KF}/KWidgetsAddons COMPONENT Devel
 )
 
-qt6_add_resources(KF6WidgetsAddons "kcharselect-data"
-    PREFIX "/kf6/kcharselect/"
-    FILES kcharselect-data
-    OUTPUT_TARGETS _rcc_target
-)
-install(TARGETS ${_rcc_target} EXPORT KF6WidgetsAddonsTargets ${KF_INSTALL_TARGETS_DEFAULT_ARGS})
+# 3.1 MB of Unicode tables, and the only thing that reads them is
+# KCharSelectData, which is excluded above. Compiling it in would put 3 MB of
+# dead weight into every KF6 wasm component that links KWidgetsAddons.
+if(NOT CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "WASI")
+    qt6_add_resources(KF6WidgetsAddons "kcharselect-data"
+        PREFIX "/kf6/kcharselect/"
+        FILES kcharselect-data
+        OUTPUT_TARGETS _rcc_target
+    )
+    install(TARGETS ${_rcc_target} EXPORT KF6WidgetsAddonsTargets ${KF_INSTALL_TARGETS_DEFAULT_ARGS})
+endif()
 
 if(BUILD_DESIGNERPLUGIN)
     add_subdirectory(designer)
